     "size": 18,
     "color": "#000000",
     "align": "left"
    }
   ],
   "speech": "RefCOCO gives you a phrase, and you mask that object. It's coarse localization: at IoU 0.6 we hit 1.8 percent, LSeg 5. Predicting the whole image gets 0.10. GroupViT gets 0.11. Peekaboo with the color filter I just showed gets 0.16. Guide the filter with depth from MiDaS instead and it's 0.20, but MiDaS is a supervised depth model, so that number isn't fully label-free. LSeg, trained on segmentation labels, wins. On VOC-C we lose to Clippy, GroupViT and LSeg; the depth variant just edges past Clippy. What it grabs can differ from the label: ask for \"Emma Watson\" and you get her face and upper body, not the dress. The prior can also overreach: with one-word prompts it sometimes carves a cat shape out of background texture. No training of our own isn't free, either: it leans on Stable Diffusion's pretraining and about two hundred U-Net passes per mask."
  },
  {
   "title": "Bonus: transparency",
   "elements": [
    {
     "kind": "image",
     "path": "/Users/rburgert/Documents/ThesisProposal/presentation/agents/.scratchpad/explain_peekaboo/final/rgba_generation_timeline.png",
     "rect": [
      100,
      100,
      760,
      382
     ]
    }
   ],
   "speech": "Make the image learnable too, not just the mask, and the same loss generates images with transparency, starting from noise. No photo here, so no bilateral filter. The checkerboard is the transparent part. Qualitative only, and Dream Fields did something similar with CLIP in 3D. Peekaboo was a CVPR 2023 workshop paper, about forty citations so far."
  }
